---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Used Peloton Treadmill (2026): Recalls, Fees and Fair Prices";
const description =
  "How to buy a used Peloton Tread or Tread+ safely: the 2021 recalls and how to confirm the fix, the $95 activation fee, the membership, what to check in person, fair prices against a new Tread Flex, and where to look.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "peloton-cross-training-tread",
    name: "Peloton Cross Training Tread",
    brand: "Peloton",
    category: "The Belt-Deck Peloton, Bought New on Amazon",
    badge: "Closest New Match to a Used Tread",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FLQQ6Q4P?tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $2,695 (Amazon, 9 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":2695,"high":2695},
    specs: "59 in. running space · about 24 in. swivel touchscreen · manual and auto-incline · 300 lb max user · 68 x 33 x 65 in. · conventional belt",
    pros: [
      "The previous-generation belt-deck Peloton, bought new rather than second-hand",
      "A manufacturer warranty and no used-equipment activation fee",
      "No recall or account-transfer checks to make, unlike a used TR02",
      "A swivel screen for off-belt strength and yoga classes",
    ],
    cons: [
      "At about $2,695 on Amazon, about $500 more than a new Tread Flex",
      "Classes still need an All-Access Membership, which the listing gives as $49.99 a month",
      "A previous-generation model since Peloton changed its range on 1 October 2026",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The way to get the belt-deck Peloton without the used-market checks: new, under warranty and with no activation fee. At Amazon’s price it costs more than a Tread Flex, so buy it only if you want this design.",
    description: "Used Peloton treadmills are, by definition, not sold new on Amazon, so this card shows the nearest thing that is: the Cross Training Tread, the belt-deck Peloton sold until the October 2026 range change, which Amazon listed new on 9 November 2026. The Amazon listing gives 59 inches of running space on a textured belt, a 24-inch HD swivel touchscreen, manual and auto-incline, speed and incline knobs, a 300 lb user limit for users from 4 ft 11 in. to 6 ft 4 in., and a 68 by 33 by 65-inch footprint, and says it needs Wi-Fi and a 120-volt, 12-amp supply; Johnson Fitness gives the same 59-inch running length on a 20-inch-wide belt, and another retailer the same 300 lb limit and a 68 by 33 by 64.5-inch footprint. Peloton's own spec pages and the CPSC give the earlier Tread's screen as 23.8 inches, so treat the screen size as about 24 inches. Bought new, it avoids everything this guide warns about with a used Tread: no touchscreen-recall check, no account release, no activation fee, and a warranty. The catch is price. At $2,694.99 on Amazon it cost about $500 more than a new Tread Flex at Peloton on 5 November, so it only makes sense if you want this design over the folding Flex. The All-Access Membership is extra; the Amazon listing gave it as $49.99 a month.",
  },
  {
    id: "peloton-tread-vision",
    name: "Peloton Tread Vision",
    brand: "Peloton",
    category: "The Current Peloton Belt-Deck Treadmill, New on Amazon",
    badge: "Instead of a Used Tread+",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HDRFM27D?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31jcwMrxC5L._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $3,500",
    priceBounds: {"low":3500,"high":3500},
    specs: "23.8 in. swivel touchscreen · 0-12.5 mph · 0-12.5% incline · non-folding",
    pros: [
      "No slat belt and no Tread+ recall history to check, bought new with a warranty",
      "A 23.8-inch screen that swivels for off-belt strength and yoga classes",
      "Movement-tracking camera for running-form and rep feedback, new on this generation",
      "Speed and incline knobs with jump buttons for instant interval changes",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Launched on 1 October 2026, so there is no long-term independent coverage of reliability yet",
      "The form-tracking, personalised plans and classes are membership features, and the membership is ongoing",
      "A conventional 59-inch belt, not the Tread+’s long slatted deck",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The new Peloton to consider if you were looking at a used Tread+ for a premium machine: no rear-guard recall to check, a warranty, and a movement-tracking camera, at a new-machine price.",
    description: "A used Tread+ is not something Amazon sells, and Peloton's current slat-belt Tread+ Vision is sold through Peloton, so this card shows the premium Peloton treadmill that is on Amazon: the Tread Vision. Peloton replaced its treadmill line in autumn 2026 with three machines: the folding Tread Flex, the Tread Vision and the slat-belt Tread+ Vision. Peloton's specification page and its launch material agree on a 23.8-inch swivel touchscreen, 0 to 12.5 mph and 0 to 12.5% incline, and the machine does not fold; Peloton lists a 59 by 20-inch cushioned belt and a 300 lb maximum user weight. Its new feature is a movement-tracking camera that scores running efficiency from cadence and vertical oscillation and counts reps in strength classes. Against a used TR01 it gives up the long slatted deck, but it comes new, with no rear-guard recall to confirm and no activation fee. Peloton prices the hardware with the All-Access Membership sold separately and says the membership is required for its content and features. Because the machine has only been on sale since 1 October, we cannot point to independent reliability coverage, and the warranty is 12 months limited, with longer belt, motor and frame guarantees. Peloton's shop listed it at $3,495 on 5 November 2026; its figures are those checked for our smart treadmill guide.",
  },
  {
    id: "peloton-tread-flex-new",
    name: "Peloton Tread Flex",
    brand: "Peloton",
    category: "The New Peloton to Price Against Any Used One",
    badge: "The New Benchmark",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Peloton+Tread+Flex&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$2,195 at Peloton (5 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":2195,"high":2195},
    specs: "0-12.5 mph · 0-12.5% incline · 21.5 in. swivel touchscreen · folding · new, with warranty",
    pros: [
      "Peloton’s cheapest treadmill, and its first that folds",
      "New, with Peloton’s warranty and no used-equipment activation fee",
      "Peloton was offering to match a better Tread Flex offer on its own site for purchases from 1 to 11 November",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Still needs an All-Access Membership for classes",
      "No movement-tracking camera, unlike the Tread Vision",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The price any used Peloton treadmill has to beat once fees and moving are added. If it does not, buy this instead.",
    description: "The Tread Flex is Peloton’s folding treadmill, launched on 1 October 2026. Our Peloton Tread guide checked its figures against Peloton and independent launch coverage: 0 to 12.5 mph, 0 to 12.5% incline, a 21.5-inch swivel touchscreen and a 290 lb machine weight, folding to 37.8 inches long. On 5 November 2026 Peloton’s US shop listed it at $2,195, with the All-Access Membership sold separately and a price-match offer for purchases made from 1 to 11 November. Peloton’s shop listed the Tread Vision at $3,495 and the Tread+ Vision at $6,695 on the same day.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "By Price", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/price/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Used Peloton Treadmill", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/used-peloton-tread/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it safe to buy a used Peloton Tread+?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only if the CPSC-approved rear guard is fitted. Every Tread+ model TR01 sold from 2018 to April 2021 was recalled in May 2021 because people, pets and objects could be pulled under the rear; a child died. The free breakaway rear guard announced in May 2023 cuts power and slows the belt on contact. Give Peloton the serial number and ask it to confirm the remedy before you buy.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does Peloton charge to activate a used treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Peloton introduced a one-time $95 used-equipment activation fee in the US in August 2024 for new members activating second-hand hardware, including the Tread and Tread+. It was waived for promotions in early 2026, most recently until 31 March 2026, and was reported at $45 for purchases on Peloton’s Repowered marketplace at its 2025 launch. Check the current figure when you activate.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much should I pay for a used Peloton treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Enough below a new Tread Flex to cover the fee, the move and the lack of a warranty. The Flex was $2,195 at Peloton on 5 November 2026. One resale marketplace reported a typical used Tread sale of about $1,800 in August 2026; add $95 for activation and $500 to $900 for delivery and that exceeds the new Flex, so negotiate accordingly.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a Peloton treadmill warranty transfer to a second owner?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Assume not. Sources describe Peloton’s limited home warranty as covering the original purchaser only, while 2022 reports said paid extended protection plans became transferable. Ask the seller whether they bought a plan, and ask Peloton what, if anything, covers that serial number.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a membership for a used Peloton Tread?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For classes and most screen features, yes: an All-Access Membership, which coverage of Peloton’s October 2025 price change put at $49.99 a month in the US. Peloton says its current Treads offer Just Run, a manual mode, without one. Check what the software on the machine you are buying offers.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I know which Peloton treadmill model I am buying?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Check the model number on the black sticker on the front end cap of the deck. TR01 is the slat-belt Tread+, sold as the Peloton Tread until September 2020; TR02 is the belt-deck Tread. A listing for a 2019 \"Peloton Tread\" is almost certainly a recalled TR01.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-05",
      dateModified: "2026-11-05",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 3,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/used-peloton-tread/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-05",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-05",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "used peloton treadmill",
      "peloton treadmill for sale",
      "peloton treadmill used",
      "used peloton treadmill for sale",
      "used peloton tread+",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "What to Check", link: "#inspection" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-usedpelotontread" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-usedpelotontread)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Used
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Buying a Used <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Peloton Treadmill</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Peloton has sold well over 100,000 treadmills in the US, and plenty are now for sale. A used one can be a good buy, but only if three things check out first: the recall remedy, the costs Peloton adds when you activate it, and a price that still beats a new Tread Flex once you have moved it home.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#recalls" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            The Recalls
          </a>
          <a href="#fair-price" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Fair Prices
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">November 5, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          5 November 2026 we read the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s two May 2021 Peloton
          treadmill recall notices (21-128 and 21-129, the latter updated August 2021) and its May
          2023 announcement of the Tread+ rear-guard repair, and searched its recall database for
          later Peloton treadmill recalls. We read Peloton’s US Tread shop and Tread Flex pages for
          current new prices and its used-bike page for its stated activation-fee terms. Peloton’s
          own fee and membership pages did not display a figure when we looked, so the fee is taken
          from Peloton’s 2024 announcement as reported by TechCrunch, NBC News and Pelo Buddy, whose
          March 2026 report of a waiver to 31 March we also read, and the Repowered terms from
          CNBC’s June 2025 report. Used prices are from Commonplace, a resale marketplace with a
          commercial interest in the figures, dated 7 October 2026; we found no independent used-price
          index for Peloton treadmills, so treat them as one marketplace’s asking prices. The
          fair-price guidance is our editorial judgement, not a valuation. We have not inspected or
          tested any used Peloton treadmill. Prices change often. On 9 November 2026 we replaced the cards for the used TR02 Tread and TR01 Tread+, which
          are not sold on Amazon, with the new Cross Training Tread, checked against its Amazon listing,
          Johnson Fitness and another retailer, and the Tread Vision, whose figures are those checked
          for our smart treadmill guide. We earn a commission if you buy
          through an Amazon link on this page; it does not change what we say. This site is not
          affiliated with Peloton or any marketplace mentioned.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>


    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <div class="not-prose bg-orange-50 border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-3">Our Top Picks</h2>
        <ul class="list-none m-0 p-0">
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Closest New Match to a Used Tread:</span>
            <a href="#peloton-cross-training-tread" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Peloton Cross Training Tread</a>
            <a href="#peloton-cross-training-tread" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Instead of a Used Tread+:</span>
            <a href="#peloton-tread-vision" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Peloton Tread Vision</a>
            <a href="#peloton-tread-vision" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">The New Benchmark:</span>
            <a href="#peloton-tread-flex-new" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Peloton Tread Flex</a>
            <a href="#peloton-tread-flex-new" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Is a Used Peloton Treadmill Worth Buying?</a>
          <a href="#which-model" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Which Peloton Treadmill Are You Looking At?</a>
          <a href="#recalls" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. The 2021 Recalls, and How to Confirm the Fix</a>
          <a href="#fees" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The Activation Fee, the Membership and the Warranty</a>
          <a href="#inspection" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. What to Check</a>
          <a href="#moving" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Moving a Used Peloton Home</a>
          <a href="#fair-price" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Fair Prices</a>
          <a href="#where-to-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Where to Buy</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. The Alternatives to a Used Peloton</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Who Should Buy a Used Peloton Treadmill</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A used Peloton treadmill looks like an easy saving: the machines are expensive new, many
          sellers bought them during 2020 and 2021 and no longer use them, and Peloton replaced its
          whole treadmill range on 1 October 2026, which has pushed more older machines onto the
          market. The saving is real only if the machine is safe, if you budget for what Peloton
          charges a second owner, and if the price still makes sense next to a new Tread Flex at
          $2,195. Both Peloton treadmill models sold before mid-2021 were recalled, one of them after
          a child died. This guide explains how to tell which machine you are looking at, how to
          confirm the recall remedy, what activation and the membership add, what to check in
          person, what to pay and where to look.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is a Used Peloton Treadmill Worth Buying?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A used Peloton treadmill is worth buying only if you can confirm the recall
            remedy, you budget for Peloton’s activation fee and membership, and the total cost —
            asking price, fee and moving — still sits clearly below a new $2,195 Tread Flex.</strong>
            When all three hold, it can be a good way into Peloton’s classes. When any one fails, a
            new machine, or a cheaper non-Peloton treadmill with the Peloton App, is the better buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            The recall check comes first and is not optional. Every Tread+ sold from 2018 to April
            2021 (model TR01) was recalled because people, pets and objects could be pulled under the
            rear of the machine; a six-year-old child died. A Tread+ without the approved rear guard
            fitted is not a machine to bring into a home at any price. About 1,050 of the regular Tread
            (model TR02) sold in the US from November 2020 to March 2021 were also recalled, because the
            touchscreen could fall off.
          </p>
          <p>
            The money check comes second. Peloton has charged new members a one-time $95 fee to activate
            second-hand hardware since 2024, classes need a monthly All-Access Membership, no
            manufacturer warranty should be assumed to transfer, and moving a 290 lb-plus treadmill is
            rarely free. On the asking prices we found, many used Treads cost about as much delivered
            as a new Tread Flex. Our
            <a href="/peloton-tread/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton Tread guide</a>
            covers the current new range in full.
          </p>
          <p>
            A used machine is not something Amazon sells, so the cards below do not show a used
            Tread or Tread+. They show the new Peloton treadmills you can order on Amazon to price a
            used one against: the previous-generation Cross Training Tread, the current Tread Vision,
            and the Tread Flex that sets the benchmark. Everything this guide says about checking a
            used Peloton applies to the private and marketplace listings, not to these cards.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Product cards -->
      <div class="not-prose">
        {
          PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
